-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+Each accepted submission is stored as one complete visit **revision**. Revisions of a visit form a tree through their base revision. A visit's *head* is a revision with no accepted child. A visit with exactly one head is **settled**; a visit with more than one head is **unresolved** and has open review work.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+
+### Submission envelope
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| visit ID | Client-generated UUID |
+| base revision ID | The revision the tablet started from; absent for the first revision of a visit |
+| idempotency key | Retry identity |
+| complete visit payload | Plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes |
+
+The payload is always the whole visit, not a set of changes.
+
+### Acceptance
+
+- A submission whose base revision is the visit's only head becomes the new head; the visit stays settled.
+- A submission whose base revision already has an accepted child, or a first revision for a visit that already has revisions, becomes an additional head; the visit becomes unresolved.
+- A submission naming a base revision that does not belong to the visit is rejected.
+
+In every case the original submission is retained.
+
+### Current-state response
+
+Retrieval returns the visit's heads and an explicit settled or unresolved indication. A settled visit's single head is its current state. An unresolved visit returns all competing heads and no single current state; the response never picks one by arrival order.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+## Review Resolution
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by naming the heads it settles. The outcome is retained with the coordinator's identity, the contributing revision IDs, and the resulting revision. Competing revisions that are not chosen stay in submission history.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: each submission names its base revision; competing revisions go to coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge: tablets send base snapshots and structured changes, disjoint field edits combine automatically and overlaps go to review | A larger review queue is accepted so that no current state is a combination nobody submitted. Full criteria, reconsideration triggers, and downstream obligations: [decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md). |
+| Unit of submission | Complete visit payload | Structured field-level changes | Keeps each worker's submitted visit intact and keeps the tablet's base state to a revision ID. |
+| Current state of an unresolved visit | No single current state; all heads returned | Return the most recently received head | Arrival order is not evidence of authority. |
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+### Deferred
+1. Representation of review work as a queue (ordering, assignment between the two coordinators) is sized with review-export.
+2. Tablet-side display of pending competing revisions is sized with tablet-sync.
 
